-- Problem Name: Rising Temperature

/*
📌 Beginner-Friendly Problem Explanation
You’re given a table `Weather(id, recordDate, temperature)` with one row per day.
Find the IDs of the dates where the temperature is higher than the temperature on the
previous day (exactly 1 day earlier).

Key facts:
- Each date appears at most once.
- Return only the `id` of the dates that are warmer than “yesterday”.
- Result can be in any order.

📊 Example Input
Weather
+----+------------+-------------+
| id | recordDate | temperature |
+----+------------+-------------+
|  1 | 2025-08-01 |     30      |
|  2 | 2025-08-02 |     35      |
|  3 | 2025-08-3  |     28      |
|  4 | 2025-08-04 |     33      |
+----+------------+-------------+

Step-by-step comparison
- 2025-08-02 (35) vs 2025-08-01 (30) → higher → keep id = 2
- 2025-08-03 (28) vs 2025-08-02 (35) → lower → discard
- 2025-08-04 (33) vs 2025-08-03 (28) → higher → keep id = 4

✅ Expected Output
+----+
| id |
+----+
|  2 |
|  4 |
+----+

🧠 Step-by-Step Approach
1) Treat the table twice (self-join): one copy as “today” (alias `a`) and one as “yesterday” (alias `b`).
2) Match rows where `a.recordDate` is exactly 1 day after `b.recordDate`.
3) Keep only those matches where `a.temperature > b.temperature`.
4) Return `a.id` (the id of the “today” row).

❓ Why we use JOIN here
- We must compare each day to the row from exactly one day earlier. Those two rows live
  in the same table. A self-join lets us line up “today” with “yesterday” on a single row,
  so we can compare their temperatures easily.
- Without a JOIN (or a window function like LAG, which isn’t always available),
  SQL can’t “look at another row” from the same table in a straightforward way.
  The self-join creates that pairwise comparison: `a` (today) ↔ `b` (yesterday).
- The condition `DATEDIFF(a.recordDate, b.recordDate) = 1` guarantees the dates are consecutive.
  Then the WHERE clause checks the temperature increase.
*/

-- Your Query with Detailed Inline Comments
SELECT
    a.id                              -- ✅ Return the id of the warmer (today) date
FROM Weather AS a                     -- a = "today" row
JOIN Weather AS b                     -- b = "yesterday" row
    ON DATEDIFF(a.recordDate, b.recordDate) = 1
                                               -- DATEDIFF(x, y) returns (x - y) in days in MySQL.
                                               -- = 1 means: a.recordDate is exactly one day after b.recordDate.
WHERE
    a.temperature > b.temperature;    -- Keep only cases where today is warmer than yesterday
